From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Mason Freed <masonf@chromium.org>
Date: Mon, 26 Oct 2026 12:55:08 -0700
Subject: Harden Element attribute mutation methods against re-entrant changes

Element::WillModifyAttribute() can run script both by pausing in the
debugger (via probe::WillModifyDOMAttr) and when UpdateName() lazily
initializes a frame's WindowProxy and synchronously invokes a
page-defined setter (e.g. via chrome.loadTimes). If script mutates the
element's attributes and reallocates or re-indexes
UniqueElementData::attribute_vector_, references and indices into the
attribute storage acquired before WillModifyAttribute() become invalid.

While https://crrev.com/c/8510124 already hardened
SetAttributeInternal() and RemoveAttributeInternal(), this CL:
1. Checks in AppendAttributeInternal() whether WillModifyAttribute()
   already added the attribute re-entrantly before appending.
2. Copies id_attr and class_attr by value in DidMoveToNewDocument()
   before calling SetIdAttribute() / setAttribute().
3. Updates comments in SetAttributeInternal() and
   RemoveAttributeInternal() and adds regression tests.

Bug: 576203565, 575317938
Change-Id: Ic63c0b5a15568a9c8d2331e7b49dde89e36b5283
Reviewed-on: https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/c/chromium/src/+/8633017
